**The strongest remaining target is a smooth ideal in a specified imaginary-quadratic class.** Public information about small split primes improved the toy yield, but a counting bound shows why that gain alone cannot give general factoring below \(L_N[1/3]\).

The [full report and 17-row reduction table](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/equiv_c/harvest.md) gives the reduction direction, exponent cost, native algorithms, and steering requirement for each route family. The [search concordance](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/equiv_c/concordance.tsv) records 537 matching lines across 77 files.

| Rank | Target | What the steering test established | Subjective probability of general sub-⅓ factoring |
|---|---|---|---:|
| 1 | Smooth representatives in \(\mathrm{Cl}(-4kN)\) | Scoring public multipliers produced **476 versus 70** smooth norms at 16-bit primes and **17 versus 5** at 24 bits, before charging selection. **Proved under GRH:** for \(B=L_d[a]\), \(d=4kN\), only a \(1/L_d[1-a,\Omega(1)]\) fraction of uniform classes can contain a \(B\)-smooth ideal of norm at most \(\sqrt d\,L_d[a]\), for \(0<a<1/2\). The opening is to find a witness in a much wider box at \(L_d[a]\) cost. [Class-root reduction](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/genusleak_results.md:136) · [M2 decoder](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/subthird_results.md:75) | 0.5% |
| 2 | One 2-isogeny neighbour modulo \(N\) | **Proved:** one can steer into modular cubics that split completely at both hidden primes. Keep the Legendre parameter private and reveal only \(j\). Away from exceptional fibres, any returned neighbour matches the private edge at exactly one prime with probability **4/9**, yielding a factor by gcd. Exhaustive validation gave **62,752/141,192**, exactly 4/9. The missing algorithm finds one root from \(j\) alone. The validation oracle used the factors. [Original planting reduction](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/conn_cmp.md:121) | 0.2% |
| 3 | An anomalous elliptic component | **Known:** if \(4p-1=Ds^2\) for a suitable small CM discriminant, multiplication by \(N\) can expose \(p\) directly. The discriminant condition with \(D\le1023\) covered only **2 of 1,982** primes in the window near \(2^{24}\). A general route needs an \(N\)-only curve sampler that reaches trace one at a hidden prime with inverse-\(L_N[a]\) probability. [Cheng](https://eprint.iacr.org/2002/109.pdf) · [Repository coverage analysis](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/nonsmooth_results.md:239) | 0.1% |

Two distinctions changed the ranking. **Unbounded navigation in \((\mathbb Z/N)^\times\)** supplies factoring and local prime-power DL. Its factor-base size and inverse success probability must both fit the desired exponent. **Zech addition**, by contrast, is equivalent to DL in its specified finite field. Fixed characteristic genuinely makes that target easy, but the harvest found no map taking a general factoring instance there while preserving the information needed to split \(N\). The elliptic point-count reduction likewise consumes a count directly and works for every good semiprime using the later quintic-extension argument. [Navigation](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/navigate_results.md:154) · [Zech](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/factorbits_results.md:140) · [Fixed-characteristic DL](https://doi.org/10.1090/jams/985) · [Point-count reduction](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/tracedata_results.md:467)

All toy results reproduced on rerun. The class-group test measured smoothness on forms with prime leading coefficient. Uniform target coverage, useful relation rank, and asymptotic savings remain unverified. [Pre-registration](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/equiv_c/prereg.txt) · [Code](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/equiv_c/toy.py) · [Counts](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/equiv_c/toy-results.txt)